1
00:00:00,05 --> 00:00:02,02
- [Instructor] Atoms are like units of state.

2
00:00:02,02 --> 00:00:04,04
They're like independent pieces of data

3
00:00:04,04 --> 00:00:07,00
that we can update and to which we can subscribe.

4
00:00:07,00 --> 00:00:10,03
So whenever there's a change in the states in an atom,

5
00:00:10,03 --> 00:00:13,08
each subscribed component is re-rendered with the new value.

6
00:00:13,08 --> 00:00:17,02
In this video, we learned to divide the state into atoms.

7
00:00:17,02 --> 00:00:20,00
So we're going to look at the syntax, which is very simple.

8
00:00:20,00 --> 00:00:23,04
It's like an object with two keys, two properties.

9
00:00:23,04 --> 00:00:26,00
So the key will contain a unique ID,

10
00:00:26,00 --> 00:00:28,02
which is used for debugging, persistence,

11
00:00:28,02 --> 00:00:30,08
and other advanced features.

12
00:00:30,08 --> 00:00:33,06
And you must also specify a default value.

13
00:00:33,06 --> 00:00:35,09
So I'm going to copy this syntax

14
00:00:35,09 --> 00:00:38,00
and we're going to go to our start projects.

15
00:00:38,00 --> 00:00:39,09
And here you're going to see that the source code

16
00:00:39,09 --> 00:00:43,03
is very familiar because you have several state variables

17
00:00:43,03 --> 00:00:45,02
but which is available at the top level

18
00:00:45,02 --> 00:00:47,07
of the root component, which isn't the best practice.

19
00:00:47,07 --> 00:00:50,06
Instead, we want to manage the state outside

20
00:00:50,06 --> 00:00:51,07
of every components

21
00:00:51,07 --> 00:00:54,01
and allow every component to subscribe

22
00:00:54,01 --> 00:00:55,09
to the different pieces of states

23
00:00:55,09 --> 00:00:58,02
which are defined with the atoms.

24
00:00:58,02 --> 00:01:00,05
So first, let's just make sure that we import

25
00:01:00,05 --> 00:01:05,04
from the library, recoil.

26
00:01:05,04 --> 00:01:07,06
That's going to be atom.

27
00:01:07,06 --> 00:01:11,01
And we're going to add our first atom right here.

28
00:01:11,01 --> 00:01:12,09
And the way we're going to proceed

29
00:01:12,09 --> 00:01:15,03
is that every local state variable

30
00:01:15,03 --> 00:01:16,09
is going to be replaced with an atom.

31
00:01:16,09 --> 00:01:18,05
So first it's going to be the list.

32
00:01:18,05 --> 00:01:21,07
So I'm going to name this one list

33
00:01:21,07 --> 00:01:24,01
and I'm going to name the key the exact same way

34
00:01:24,01 --> 00:01:25,04
which will be list.

35
00:01:25,04 --> 00:01:26,05
And for the default value,

36
00:01:26,05 --> 00:01:29,03
it's going to be this array of items.

37
00:01:29,03 --> 00:01:30,06
I'm going to create another item

38
00:01:30,06 --> 00:01:35,04
which will be to then manage the state for all.

39
00:01:35,04 --> 00:01:37,05
And here for the features.

40
00:01:37,05 --> 00:01:39,01
Basically we have two type of lists.

41
00:01:39,01 --> 00:01:41,02
One which is to display the full list,

42
00:01:41,02 --> 00:01:43,07
and another list which is to display the list

43
00:01:43,07 --> 00:01:45,06
of items filtered.

44
00:01:45,06 --> 00:01:47,07
And we have another variable here

45
00:01:47,07 --> 00:01:50,03
which is used to indicate when the list is filtered.

46
00:01:50,03 --> 00:01:53,01
So I'm going to define another atom

47
00:01:53,01 --> 00:01:55,07
to manage this one separately.

48
00:01:55,07 --> 00:01:59,03
So that's going to be filterStates.

49
00:01:59,03 --> 00:02:02,01
And for this one, we have a default value, which is false.

50
00:02:02,01 --> 00:02:06,01
So I'm going to do just like this one, it's going to be false.

51
00:02:06,01 --> 00:02:08,05
And last, we have also an input value.

52
00:02:08,05 --> 00:02:11,02
So I'm going to define this one below.

53
00:02:11,02 --> 00:02:14,00
And this one, I'm going to actually name this one

54
00:02:14,00 --> 00:02:14,09
a bit differently.

55
00:02:14,09 --> 00:02:20,07
It's going to be inputValue for this atom.

56
00:02:20,07 --> 00:02:23,07
And then for the default value is going to be an empty string,

57
00:02:23,07 --> 00:02:25,00
just like this one.

58
00:02:25,00 --> 00:02:26,06
So we're not going to remove that just yet.

59
00:02:26,06 --> 00:02:28,08
So that's going to be actually part two.

60
00:02:28,08 --> 00:02:30,05
We have just created atoms

61
00:02:30,05 --> 00:02:33,00
in order to contain pieces of state,

62
00:02:33,00 --> 00:02:34,05
and this is just step one.

63
00:02:34,05 --> 00:02:36,08
So the thing to keep in mind is that atoms

64
00:02:36,08 --> 00:02:40,00
contain the source of truth of your application state.

65
00:02:40,00 --> 00:02:42,00
Next we're going to learn more about recoil,

66
00:02:42,00 --> 00:02:45,00
and learn about how to read the state from the components.

